# Intern guide: closing the speed gap

This guide explains one milestone of the IR Workbench project: making the code this compiler produces execute fewer instructions, so that it comes closer to the reference compiler from the PicoCalc toolchain. The compiler today is correct and small; it is also slower than the reference by a measured margin, for reasons that have been identified one by one. The milestone adds four transformations that address those reasons, and it does so without changing any number that the project's book or its tests depend on.

You should be able to read this guide without having read anything else in the repository, although the IRWB-002 intern guide (`ttmp/2026/11/24/IRWB-002--research-agenda-…/design-doc/01-…md`) goes into more depth on the framework. Part 1 explains the parts of the system that decide how many instructions a program executes. Part 2 shows where the instructions go today, with the measurements. Part 3 is the design of the four changes. Part 4 is the implementation and validation plan, with a reference of files and functions.

Three conventions hold throughout.

- **Paths are relative to the repository root.** A number after a colon is a line number at commit `420d8e1`, the commit this milestone starts from. Line numbers drift; the symbol names next to them do not.
- **Numbers are measurements with a source.** Each one names the test, script or diary step it comes from. A number derived by counting instructions in a listing is called an *estimate* and shows the count.
- **"Proposed" marks what does not exist yet.** Everything in Part 1 and Part 2 exists in the code at `420d8e1`. The designs in Part 3 are what this milestone builds.

# Part 1: The parts of the system that decide speed

## 1. What the milestone is for

The compiler translates **Typed**, a statically typed subset of JavaScript, into machine code for **RV32**, the 32-bit RISC-V instruction set of the PicoCalc's processor. It is built on a framework, IR Workbench v6, in which every version of the program is an immutable artifact, every optimization is a pass with declared reads and writes, and every pass application is accepted only if the new program still means what the original meant. Section 4 explains that last check; it is the reason the transformations in this guide can be written without fear of silently producing a wrong program.

A second compiler exists for the same language: the reference compiler, vendored from the ds-js-os repository as `vendor/ds-js-os/compiler/typed/typed-rv32.js`. This project uses it only as an *oracle*: its images are run on the same inputs to check our evaluator and our images, and its instruction counts are the yardstick for ours. On the book's benchmarks, measured on the emulator with one fixed input each (`tests/typed/backend.test.mjs:511–515`; reproduced by `scripts/01-benchmark-probe.mjs`, output in `various/06-benchmarks-o2.txt`):

| benchmark | r0 (no passes) | ours after O2 | reference |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| Blend | 531 | 451 | 383 |
| Checksum | 446 | 366 | 292 |
| insertion sort | 2,022 | 2,022 | 1,533 |
| Collatz | 1,590 | 1,590 | 1,486 |

On Candela, the 332-function path tracer that is the project's large program, the gap is of the same order. Over 100 generated cases per function, our O2 image executes 870.3 instructions per case against 747.7 for the reference compiler's `kernel` policy (IRWB-001, `various/experiments/f32-backend-01-candela-images.md`). Rendering a scene with a program specialized to that scene, ours takes 10,435 instructions per pixel on the scene B-01 and 22,851 on R-01, against 8,501 and 15,656 for `kernel` (IRWB-001 diary step 27).
Three conventions hold throughout.

- **Paths are relative to the repository root.** A number after a colon is a line number at commit `420d8e1`, which this guide was written against. Line numbers drift; the symbol named next to them does not.
- **Part 1 describes code that exists.** Interfaces that do not exist yet are marked **Proposed**. Their names are the ones the implementation is expected to use, but an implementer may refine them.
- **The agenda items are those of the IRWB-002 guide** (`ttmp/2026/11/24/IRWB-002--research-agenda-*/design-doc/01-*.md`, Part 2). This guide is self-contained, but that document has the wider context.

# Part 1: The parts of the system you need

## 1. The compiler in one page

The IR Workbench compiles **Typed**, a statically typed subset of JavaScript, to **RV32** machine code (32-bit RISC-V). A vendored front end parses and type-checks the source and emits stack-machine code; `src/typed/lift.ts` lifts that into the main intermediate representation, `typed.blocks`. Optimization passes then turn one revision of the program into the next, and a backend (`src/typed/backend/`) lowers the final revision to an image of machine code.

```text
 main.typed ──front end──► tape ──lift──► typed.blocks r0
                                              │ pass ──► r1 ──► r2 ──► … ──► rN
                                              │        each checked against r0
                                              ▼
                                         rv32.image  (machine code)
```

Two properties of this compiler matter more than any other for this milestone.

The first is that **every pass application is checked**. The framework applies a pass only through `applyPass` (`src/framework/passes/define-pass.ts:190`), which diffs the result against its input, verifies that the pass changed only the kinds of entity it declared, runs the **meaning check** (the new revision must behave like r0 on generated inputs), and verifies the pass's claims about which analyses it leaves valid. A pass that produces a wrong program is rejected, and nothing is published.

The second is that **O2 and the book's numbers are frozen**. The corpus (`corpus/projects/`, 44 projects, 70 functions) comes with a book (`corpus/book/`) from the prototype this compiler was ported from, and the book states exact numbers: revision counts, statement counts, image sizes, instruction counts, and the provenance tree of particular statements node for node. Those numbers are tests. The standard pipeline `O2` must keep producing exactly the same revisions. Four commands guard this; Part 5 lists them. Everything this milestone adds must either reproduce today's revisions bit for bit (B1) or appear in a new pass and a new pipeline (A2).

## 2. `typed.blocks`: what a program is

`typed.blocks` is a **control-flow graph whose statements hold expression trees**. Its types are in `src/typed/model.ts`; its validator and its adapter to the framework are in `src/typed/ir.ts`.
- an **edit validator** that accepts the edits made by verified rules exactly, by re-deriving each changed expression from its old form and the rule data, with no generated input at all;
- a **regional obligation**: what the validator cannot derive is the **residual**, and only the inputs that can reach the residual are run, under version 2 of the sampled check;
- an **evidence-strength order** that says, for each revision, how strong the weakest evidence it relied on is;
- **pass variants** that cite the rules they applied in the program's provenance, alongside the frozen O2 pipeline rather than inside it;
- a **contract version** that combines the exact part and the sampled residual, and a rule for which pipelines use which contract.

Two measurements made for this guide carry the argument. Every one of the 1,232 edits that `simplify` and `fold` made on the corpus pipelines and on Candela's O2 could be re-derived exactly from its before-tree and the rule tables, by a matcher written independently of the passes (sections 11–12). Re-deriving all 838 such edits on Candela took 22 ms, where the sampled checks of the same three pass applications took 6.2 s (section 14). The same measurements also set the limit of the idea: those edits are 59.8 % of the expression-level edits on the corpus but only 18.6 % of all edits, because most of what `cfg`, `dce`, `licm` and `subst` do is structural.

## How to read this guide

Part 1 explains the parts of the system the design touches, from the code; read it in order, since each section uses the terms of the one before. Part 2 presents the measurements made for this guide. Part 3 is the design. Part 4 covers risks, what would falsify the approach, future work and related work. Part 5 is the implementation plan with its acceptance measurements.

Four conventions hold throughout.

- **Paths are relative to the repository root.** A number after a colon is a line number at commit `a4beda6`, the base of this ticket. Line numbers drift; the symbol named next to them does not.
- **IRWB-005 is not in this ticket's base.** It was merged into `irwb-001/import-v6-prototype` after `a4beda6`. Its code and evidence are cited at its final head, `bff08a6`, and its files are marked "(IRWB-005)". They were read, not run. Its source did not change between `d9cf4e9`, where this guide's reading started, and `bff08a6`; its final measurements did, and the guide uses the final ones.
- **Part 1 describes code that exists.** Interfaces that do not exist yet are marked **Proposed**; their names are the ones the implementation is expected to use, but an implementer may refine them.
- **Every number has a source:** a script of this ticket with its output in `various/`, a test, or a diary step of another ticket with its commit.

The whole proposal in one picture, explained in Part 3:

```text
   r(k-1) ────────────── pass ──────────────► r(k)          (both revisions, hashed)
      │                                         │
      └──────────► deriveRevision(r(k-1), r(k), verified rule tables)          Proposed
                        │                                  │
          every change of f re-derived         some change of f not re-derived
                   "derived"                              "residual"
                        │                                  │
